{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 33, :comments [{:quotes [{:he "ויישם", :translit "va-yasem", :gloss "and he placed"}], :mechanisms [{:kind :other, :note "Masoretic note: the word ויישם is written, indicating an anomalous spelling that signals a hidden danger in the narrative."}], :frame {:text "The midrashic tradition records that Eliezer was served a death potion by Betuel's household. This tradition is found in Bereshit Rabbah 60:8.", :sources ["Bereshit Rabbah 60:8"]}} {:quotes [{:he "ויישם", :translit "va-yasem", :gloss "and he placed"} {:he "ויישם בארון", :translit "va-yasem ba-aron", :gloss "and he placed in a box"} {:he "ב' דסמיכי הכא ואידך ויישם בארון", :translit "bet dis'mikhei haka ve-idakh va-yasem ba-aron", :gloss "two adjacent forms, here and the other 'and he placed in a box'"} {:he "לא אוכל עד אם דברתי דברי", :translit "lo okhal ad im dibarti devarai", :gloss "I will not eat until I have spoken my words"} {:he "כי עבד אברהם אנכי", :translit "ki eved avraham anokhi", :gloss "for Abraham's servant am I"}], :mechanisms [{:kind :masorah, :count 2, :occurrences ["ויישם" "ויישם בארון"]}], :frame {:text "Bereshit Rabbah 60:8 records that Betuel's household placed a death potion in Eliezer's bowl, but an angel came and switched it so that Betuel ate it and died. The same midrashic passage records that Betuel himself ate of it and died that night. Bereshit Rabbah 60:7 records Eliezer's refusal to eat until he had spoken his words. The practice of reciting blessings over food including netilat yadayim and birkat ha-motzi is addressed in Mishnah Berakhot 6:1 and Mishnah Yadayim end chapters.", :sources ["Bereshit Rabbah 60:7" "Bereshit Rabbah 60:8" "Mishnah Berakhot 6:1"]}}], :arithmetic [{:kind :other, :note "Masoretic note: the word ויישם is written, indicating an anomalous spelling that signals a hidden danger in the narrative.", :by :selah, :ran? false} {:kind :masorah, :count 2, :occurrences ["ויישם" "ויישם בארון"], :by :selah, :located [{:phrase "ויישם", :refs ["G